.TH BUNDLE 1 "2026-10-02" "bundle" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
bundle \- package a Bench application as one native executable
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bundle build
.BI \-o " APP"
.BI \-runtime " PREFIX"
.RI [ \-tool " NAME ..."]
.I APP_DIR
.br
.B APP
.RI [ \-w " WORKSPACE"]
.RI [ \-\- ]
.I GOAL
.br
.B APP
.RI [ \-w " WORKSPACE"]
.B <
.I GOAL_FILE
.br
.B APP \-c
.I WORKSPACE
.RI [ \-\- ]
.I GOAL
.br
.B APP \-resume
.I WORKSPACE
.br
.B APP
.BR \-info " | " \-help " | " \-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
Bundle embeds an application adapter, its explicitly selected reusable files,
and independently built Bench component packages in one native executable.
The generated command extracts those files and invokes the adapter through an
ordinary foreground process boundary. Existing Bench programs retain worker
execution, provider access, conversations, checks, and team coordination.
.PP
The builder requires Go on the build host. The generated executable requires
neither Go nor a Bench checkout on its recipient. Build separately for each
operating system and architecture. Builds perform no dependency downloads.
Credentials and application-specific system dependencies remain external.
.SH BUILD OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " APP"
Write a new native application executable at APP.
.TP
.BI \-runtime " PREFIX"
Use installed independent packages beneath
.IR PREFIX/lib/bench-tools .
Each component has its own package.json receipt and program assets.
.TP
.BI \-tool " NAME"
Include the named component package. Repeat to select several packages.
The default includes all installed component packages in PREFIX.
.SH APPLICATION SOURCE
APP_DIR contains app.json with schema 1, name, description, entry, files,
requires, optional interface, followup, and resume fields. Entry is a required
relative path to
an executable regular file included in the explicit files inventory.
No directory glob or implicit team entry is used. Application payloads must
not contain symlinks or special files.
.PP
Requires lists executable names checked through the final runtime PATH.
Followup and resume declare the adapter's supported continuation modes.
Source should be a clean pinned worker or assembled team export with its
original lock and member definitions. Current goals, credentials, outputs,
transcripts, and runtime memory do not belong in a package.
.SH APPLICATION ARGUMENT INTERFACE
The optional interface field is goal by default. The generated command options
and goal adapter contract below describe that interface.
.PP
Setting interface to argv preserves the entry application's own command syntax.
Every argument, stdin, and the caller's working directory pass through
unchanged.
Bundle uses the same verified extraction, executable lookup, dependency checks,
stream/status preservation, and signal forwarding. It supplies no synthetic
mode argument, goal file, workspace, mutable state, run binding, or active
marker.
The application owns help, directory selection, concurrency, evidence,
continuation, and crash recovery. Followup and resume must be absent or false.
.PP
The entry receives BUNDLE_ROOT, the extracted application directory, and
BUNDLE_ID, the package identity. Inherited BUNDLE_WORKSPACE, BUNDLE_INPUT,
BUNDLE_WORK, BUNDLE_OUTPUT, BUNDLE_STATE, BUNDLE_CONTROL, and BUNDLE_GOAL_FILE
are removed.
.PP
Only sole --bundle-info and --bundle-version arguments are reserved for package
metadata without extraction or entry execution. Alongside other arguments these
strings pass through literally. All other flags belong to the application.
.SH GENERATED COMMAND OPTIONS
Flags precede one quoted goal argument. Use
.B \-\-
for a goal beginning with a hyphen.
.TP
.BI \-w " WORKSPACE"
Select a workspace for a new run. Accept a new directory, an empty directory,
or a directory containing only staged input/. Create input/, working/, and
output/ automatically. Existing working/ or output/ directories, even empty,
prevent fresh admission. If omitted, create and retain ./NAME-RANDOM and print
its location on stderr. The adapter runs in working/.
.TP
.BI \-o " WORKSPACE"
Compatibility alias for -w, with the same three-folder layout. Do not supply
both selectors. The builder's -o still selects its executable destination.
.TP
.BI \-c " WORKSPACE"
Explicitly select a prior run and admit a new goal message. Requires the
application's followup capability. This is not inferred from an existing
directory or the most recent invocation.
.TP
.BI \-resume " WORKSPACE"
Explicitly resume the last immutable goal for the selected run. Accepts no
new goal and requires the application's resume capability.
.TP
.B \-info
Print the embedded package manifest as JSON without running the application.
.TP
.B \-help
Print generated command usage.
.TP
.B \-version
Print generated command version information.
.SH STREAMS
With a positional goal, nonterminal stdin is evidence passed to the adapter.
Without a positional goal, nonterminal stdin supplies the goal. The launcher
does not read goals from a terminal or enter interactive chat.
.PP
Adapter stdout and stderr remain separate ordinary streams. Launcher paths
and diagnostics go to stderr. The application defines its useful stdout
format and file deliverables. Selecting a workspace does not change
stdout into a status envelope.
.SH ADAPTER CONTRACT
Entry receives exactly one argument: run, follow, or resume. Its working
directory is the workspace working/ folder. Goals are supplied through a private file,
not through adapter argv. The adapter maps this contract to its existing team
entry command and must preserve that team's handoff and acceptance rules.
.TP
.B BUNDLE_ROOT
Extracted application definition directory.
.TP
.B BUNDLE_WORKSPACE
Selected workspace root, also used by -c and -resume.
.TP
.B BUNDLE_INPUT
Workspace input/ directory for supplied source material.
.TP
.B BUNDLE_WORK
Workspace working/ directory for drafts and intermediate work; also cwd.
.TP
.B BUNDLE_OUTPUT
Workspace output/ directory for deliverables published after adapter checks.
.TP
.B BUNDLE_STATE
Separate mutable application state directory.
.TP
.B BUNDLE_CONTROL
Controller records outside writable work and state.
.TP
.B BUNDLE_GOAL_FILE
Private file containing the invocation goal.
.PP
Runtime PATH searches the extracted runtime bin before the absolute entries
of the caller's PATH. Empty and relative entries are dropped before both
dependency checks and execution, avoiding changed lookup after entering the
working directory. Use absolute paths for explicit executable selectors.
Caller-selected provider and tool settings retain their existing meaning.
Bundle grants no networking, filesystem, or effect authority to the adapter.
.SH FILES
Private package data and run state live beneath
.IR ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/bundle/PACKAGE_ID/ .
Run directories beneath runs are identified by the physical workspace path hash.
Extracted definitions/runtime, mutable state, controller evidence, and the
external workspace remain separate.
.PP
Keep private state and the workspace for continuation. Moving a workspace or rebuilding a changed package does not implicitly migrate a run.
There is no separate run-directory option or implicit latest-session selector.
Continuation requires all three layout directories and never recreates a missing
one. Symlinks in place of the selected root or its layout directories are refused.
.PP
Input selection and preservation, task tracking, checks, and publication remain
adapter responsibilities. Bundle does not copy stdin into input/, make inputs
read-only, freeze their contents, or certify files placed in output/.
Scope worker write grants to working/ and appropriate state, and publish only
accepted artifacts through the adapter.
.PP
Existing binaries retain their original layout. Rebuilt goal adapters must
publish accepted files through BUNDLE_OUTPUT; BUNDLE_WORK now selects working/.
Changed package identities do not migrate old run state.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The generated command returns the adapter's numeric status unchanged.
Common Agent/Ply statuses are 0 accepted, 1 broken, 2 unfinished, 3 declined,
75 waiting, 125 uncertain or boundary failure, and 130 interrupted.
Other applications retain their documented status meanings.
.PP
Bundle never retries automatically. Diagnose uncertain effects before an
explicit resume. A checkpoint preserves conversation context, not exactly-once
execution or proof that repeating an effect is safe.
.SH CRASH RECOVERY
For the goal interface, before execution, Bundle writes active.json beside the
selected run's control
directory. It removes this marker after observing the adapter's termination.
If the launcher stops without confirming the end, a retained marker blocks
subsequent continuation with status 125. The diagnostic names the marker.
The adapter or its children may still be running.
.PP
Inspect retained records, live processes, and external effects. Establish that
the prior invocation has stopped and determine whether repetition is safe.
Only then remove the exact marker named in the diagnostic and explicitly
select a follow-up or resume. Removing the marker does not establish task
completion or resolve an uncertain external effect.
.SH NOTES
A follow-up must be checked against its current request. For an Agent adapter,
a named checkpoint retains conversation context and -B starts work despite
an old passing pre-check; acceptance must still bind output to current inputs.
Existing teams with immutable briefs or structured intake require their own
adapters. Packaging alone does not make them conversational.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR agent (1),
.BR ask (1),
.BR ply (1),
.BR record (1)
